description:
Found while writing the Ave House demo. The compiler retunes every warped clip to A440 by negating the manifest's tonal.tuning_cents (compile.rs ~1825, ~1983, ~2082). For many of the 53 ccMixter imports that value is wrong. Several read exactly -23.0 (Ave_34409, Drive_43098, The-Power, Once, Hornet), which looks like an estimator hitting a bin edge. An independent librosa.estimate_tuning measures them within a few cents of A440. Measured differences (manifest minus librosa, in cents): Ave -26, Drive -23, Where-You-Are-Now -16, CSoul +12, L-T-H-AA-s-Refix +17, Once -15, Hornet -18, Mine-Yours-Ours -26, Copyright +40, Dear-Mr-Williams +27. The marine-band stems agree to within 2. Effect: a render of Ave loops measures 23 cents sharp (check-render: A = 444 Hz), and mixing two ccMixter sources puts them up to about 50 cents apart, which is audibly discordant. The fix belongs in analysis/apricity_analyze (tuning estimate):
- use a robust estimate on the harmonic component (for example HPSS, then librosa.estimate_tuning or essentia with finer resolution), cross-checked;
- when estimators disagree by more than 10 cents, store 0 and flag it;
- re-run the tuning for all tracked manifests without re-analyzing everything else, as markup --holds does.
Add a test on synthetic tones detuned by +/-20 cents, and a regression check that no manifest sits at a bin-edge value. Consider also a clip option (tune <cents>) to override.
